the government's scientific advisory  committee sage has released its latest  estimate of the r number that critical  measure of how fast the virus is  spreading  is now between 1.3 and 1.5 more or less  the same as last week  the epidemic is also growing by between  four percent and seven percent  every day now what does that really mean  well the weekly study by the office of  national statistics gives the most  reliable snapshot of the epidemic it  tests random people  whether or not they've had symptoms the  latest data  for the first week of october shows  almost 28  000 people a day were being infected up  from  a little over 17 000 the week before  it estimates that one in 160 people had  the virus  up from one in 240 the previous week  a ryzen infection leads to more hospital  admissions around two to three weeks  later  now the latest government stats show 844  people  have been admitted to the uk hospitals  over a 24-hour period  that's up from 603 a week earlier  but the pressure is building faster in  the hotspot  so in the northwest admissions are up 73  in a week with doctors warning beds will  soon be full  in the northeastern yorkshire they're up  49  and in london they're up by 55 but  elsewhere  the epidemic is in a very different gear  with much lower admission rates  to get the r number below one and shrink  the epidemic  the government needs to reduce the  contact between people  whether that's through its three-tier  system or a circuit breaker  but the office for national statistics  has released another report with some  worrying data  two weeks after the prime minister urged  people to work from home again wherever  possible  to a proportion of people commuting  actually increased  to 65 the highest since lockdown started  easing  at the end of may now the message isn't  getting through  more mixing of people means more spread  of the virus  and that really doesn't bode well for  the future
 

